  // Geography
  g1: 'Canberra was built as a compromise when Sydney and Melbourne both wanted to be capital. Parliament first met there in 1927.',
  g2: 'The Pacific covers about a third of Earth\'s surface, more than all the land combined. Magellan named it "peaceful" in 1520.',
  g3: 'Queen Victoria chose Ottawa in 1857, partly because it sat between English Toronto and French Montreal and was harder to attack.',
  g4: 'The Sahara spans much of North Africa and is about the size of the United States. It is the world\'s largest hot desert.',
  g5: 'Vatican City covers about 110 acres inside Rome, smaller than many city parks. It became an independent state in 1929.',
  g6: 'Everest straddles Nepal and the Tibet region of China. Climbers can approach from the Nepal south side or the Tibet north side.',
  g7: 'The U.S. has had 50 states since 1959, when Alaska and then Hawaii joined. The flag\'s 50-star design dates to 1960.',
  g8: 'The single star recalls Texas\'s years as an independent republic, 1836 to 1845, and appears on its state flag.',
  g101: 'Tokyo, once called Edo, became the capital in 1868 when the emperor moved there from Kyoto. Its name means "eastern capital."',
  g102: 'The Nile runs north through Cairo to the Mediterranean. At about 4,100 miles, it is usually listed as the world\'s longest river.',
  g103: 'Lady Liberty, a gift from France dedicated in 1886, stands on Liberty Island in New York Harbor, greeting ships entering the port.',
  g104: 'Hawaii is a chain of volcanic islands in the Pacific, about 2,400 miles from California. It became the 50th state in 1959.',
  g105: 'Alaska is more than twice the size of Texas. The U.S. bought it from Russia in 1867 for $7.2 million, about 2 cents an acre.',
  g106: 'Mount Rushmore is in South Dakota\'s Black Hills. Its four 60-foot presidential faces were carved from 1927 to 1941.',
  g107: 'The Grand Canyon is in northern Arizona, giving it the nickname the Grand Canyon State. It is about a mile deep in places.',
  g108: 'Lake Superior is the largest freshwater lake on Earth by surface area. To remember the lakes, use HOMES: Huron, Ontario, Michigan, Erie, Superior.',
  g109: 'Florida is the Sunshine State, adopted officially in 1970. Its warm climate made it a top spot for retirees and tourists.',
  g110: 'The Golden Gate Bridge opened in 1937 over the strait at the mouth of San Francisco Bay. Its color is called "International Orange."',
  g111: 'Canada\'s red maple leaf flag was adopted in 1965, replacing a design that included the British Union Jack.',
  g112: 'Toronto is the capital of Ontario and Canada\'s largest city. Ontario is also home to the national capital, Ottawa.',
  g113: 'Stonehenge stands on Salisbury Plain in southern England. Its large stones were raised around 2500 BC.',
  g114: 'New Delhi was built by the British and became India\'s capital in 1931, replacing Calcutta, now Kolkata.',
  g115: 'Oslo is Norway\'s capital and largest city. It awards the Nobel Peace Prize each December; the other Nobels are given in Stockholm.',
  g116: 'The Indian Ocean lies between Africa to the west and Australia to the east. It is the third-largest ocean after the Pacific and Atlantic.',
  g117: 'Ataturk made Ankara the capital in 1923, choosing a central city over Istanbul, the old Ottoman capital.',
  g118: 'Brasilia was built from scratch in the country\'s interior and became capital in 1960, replacing Rio. Its layout resembles an airplane.',
  g119: 'Albany, on the Hudson River, has been New York\'s capital since 1797. Many states put their capitals away from their largest city.',
  g120: 'The U.S.-Canada border runs about 5,500 miles, counting Alaska. Most of the length is the land and lake line across the continent.',
  g121: 'The Rio Grande forms the Texas-Mexico border from El Paso to the Gulf of Mexico. In Mexico it is called the Rio Bravo.',
  g122: 'Maine touches only New Hampshire. The rest of its border is Canada and the Atlantic Ocean.',
  g123: 'At the Four Corners, Arizona, Colorado, New Mexico and Utah meet at one point, the only place in the U.S. where four states touch.',
  g124: 'Denali in Alaska rises 20,310 feet. It was named Mount McKinley from 1917 to 2015, and the federal name changed back to McKinley in 2025.',
  g125: 'Machu Picchu sits high in the Andes of Peru. Built by the Inca in the 1400s, it was largely unknown to the outside world until 1911.',
  g126: 'Only about 9 miles of water separate Spain from Morocco at the strait, the narrow gateway between the Atlantic and the Mediterranean.',
  g127: 'Buenos Aires, meaning "fair winds," sits on the Rio de la Plata. Santiago is Chile\'s capital and Montevideo is Uruguay\'s.',
  g128: 'The Urals run north to south through Russia, and are the usual line between Europe and Asia. Russia spans both continents.',
  g129: 'Kilimanjaro, Africa\'s tallest peak at about 19,341 feet, rises in northern Tanzania, just south of the Kenya border.',
  g130: 'Minnesota actually has more than 11,000 lakes of 10 acres or more. Lakes are so common that many are named simply Mud or Long Lake.',
  g131: 'Over millions of years, the Colorado River cut the canyon about a mile deep through rock layers. It still flows along the bottom today.',
  g132: 'The "Show Me" nickname is linked to Missouri congressman Willard Vandiver, who said in 1899, "I\'m from Missouri, and you\'ve got to show me."',
  g133: 'Alaska has more ocean coastline than all other states combined, about 6,600 miles. Florida has the longest coastline in the lower 48.',
  g134: 'The Red Sea separates Arabia from Africa and links to the Mediterranean through the Suez Canal, opened in 1869.',
  g135: 'Wellington, at the south tip of the North Island, became the capital in 1865. Auckland is the largest city, but not the capital.',
  g136: 'Bolivia lost its Pacific coast to Chile in a war that began in 1879. It still keeps a navy, which patrols Lake Titicaca.',
  g137: 'The Dead Sea lies between Israel and the West Bank on one side and Jordan on the other. Its shore is the lowest land on Earth.',
  g138: 'About 96% of Yellowstone is in Wyoming, with slivers in Montana and Idaho. Created in 1872, it was the world\'s first national park.',
  g139: 'The Bering Strait is only about 50 miles wide. In it, Russia\'s Big Diomede and Alaska\'s Little Diomede islands sit under 3 miles apart.',
  g140: 'The Amazon starts in the Peruvian Andes, but most of its course runs across Brazil. It carries more water than any other river.',
  g141: 'When South Sudan split from Sudan in 2011, Sudan shrank, and Algeria became Africa\'s largest country. Most of it is Sahara.',
  g142: 'The Danube flows about 1,770 miles from Germany to the Black Sea, passing through four capitals, including Vienna and Budapest.',
  g143: 'Lebanon\'s flag shows a green cedar between red stripes. The cedar of Lebanon is an ancient symbol, mentioned many times in the Bible.',
  g144: 'Bhutan\'s name in its own language, Druk Yul, means "Land of the Thunder Dragon," and the white dragon on its flag honors that.',
  g145: 'Bern is Switzerland\'s "federal city" and seat of government, though Zurich is bigger. Albert Einstein worked in Bern in 1905.',
  g146: 'Frankfort is the capital, though Louisville and Lexington are larger. It was chosen in 1792, partly as a compromise between the two.',
  g147: 'Montpelier, with under 9,000 people, is the least populous U.S. state capital. Burlington is Vermont\'s largest city.',
  g148: 'Victoria Falls lies on the Zambezi River between Zambia and Zimbabwe. Its local name, Mosi-oa-Tunya, means "the smoke that thunders."',
  g149: 'Carson City was named for frontiersman Kit Carson. Many states, Nevada included, did not make their biggest city the capital.',
  g150: 'Juneau can only be reached by air or sea; no roads connect it to the rest of North America. It became the capital in 1906.',

