  wh7: 'The "unsinkable" liner hit an iceberg on its maiden voyage and sank on April 15, 1912, with more than 1,500 lives lost.',
  wh8: 'Rome finished it in AD 80 under Emperor Titus. Its original name was the Flavian Amphitheatre, after the ruling family.',
  wh101: 'Tutankhamun was a minor boy-king, which is likely why his tomb was overlooked by robbers and found packed with treasure, including his gold mask.',
  wh102: 'Volcanic ash sealed Pompeii so well that its streets, homes and even body shapes survive. Pliny the Younger wrote an eyewitness account.',
  wh103: 'The Bastille was a royal prison and arsenal, so its fall became a symbol of the French Revolution. The French call the holiday simply "le 14 juillet."',
  wh104: 'Hiroshima was bombed on August 6, 1945. Nagasaki was hit three days later, and Japan announced its surrender within a week.',
  wh105: 'Born Temüjin, he was proclaimed Genghis Khan in 1206. His grandson Kublai Khan later ruled China.',
  wh106: 'Elizabeth I, the "Virgin Queen," reigned 1558 to 1603. Her Armada-era speech to troops at Tilbury is one of the most famous in English history.',
  wh107: 'Backed by Ferdinand and Isabella, Columbus landed in the Bahamas on October 12, 1492, believing he had reached Asia.',
  wh108: 'Cleopatra VII and Antony lost to Octavian at the Battle of Actium in 31 BC. Octavian went on to become Augustus, Rome\'s first emperor.',
  wh109: 'Hadrian\'s Wall ran about 73 miles from coast to coast, marking the northern edge of the empire. Long stretches still stand.',
  wh110: 'Gandhi led campaigns of peaceful protest like the 1930 Salt March. India gained independence in 1947.',
